.TH IFCONFIG 8 2026-09-25 "Substrate" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
ifconfig \- display and configure network interfaces
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ifconfig
.RB [ \-a ]
.br
.B ifconfig
.I interface
.br
.B ifconfig
.I interface command
.RI [ command ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
With no arguments, or with the single argument
.BR \-a ,
.B ifconfig
lists every interface the kernel reports through
.BR SIOCGIFCONF ,
at most 16, separated by blank lines.
.PP
With a single
.I interface
argument it shows only that interface.
.PP
The display for an interface consists of:
.IP \(bu 2
a header line with the interface name, the flags word in decimal
followed by the names of the set flags among
.BR UP ,
.BR BROADCAST ,
.BR LOOPBACK ,
.B RUNNING
and
.BR PROMISC ,
and the MTU;
.IP \(bu
an
.B ether
line with the hardware address;
.IP \(bu
an
.B inet
line with the IPv4 address, netmask (also as a prefix length) and
broadcast address, if an IPv4 address is set;
.IP \(bu
a
.B gateway
line with the IPv4 default gateway, if one is set;
.IP \(bu
an
.B inet6
line with one IPv6 address and its prefix length, if one is set.
The address is printed as eight colon-separated hexadecimal groups
without zero compression.
.PP
Lines whose information the kernel does not provide are omitted.
.PP
When one or more
.I command
arguments follow the interface name, they are applied left to right,
each with its own
.BR ioctl (2)
call, and nothing is printed.
Processing stops at the first failing command; commands before it
remain in effect.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B up
Set the
.B IFF_UP
flag.
.TP
.B down
Clear the
.B IFF_UP
flag.
.TP
.I address
Set the IPv4 address.
Any argument that is not one of the other command keywords is taken
as an IPv4 address in dotted-quad form.
.TP
.BI netmask " mask"
Set the IPv4 netmask, given in dotted-quad form.
.TP
.BI broadcast " address"
Set the IPv4 broadcast address.
.TP
.BI mtu " n"
Set the MTU.
.I n
is converted with
.BR atoi (3)
and is not otherwise checked.
.TP
.BI "hw ether " mac
Set the hardware address;
.I mac
is six colon-separated hexadecimal octets.
.TP
.BI gateway " address"
Set the IPv4 default gateway.
This is a Substrate extension
.RB ( SIOCSIFGATEWAY ).
.TP
.BI "inet6 add " address\c
.RI [/ prefix ]
Add an IPv6 address.
The prefix length defaults to 64.
.TP
.BI "inet6 del " address
Delete an IPv6 address.
.TP
.BI "inet6 gw " address
Set the IPv6 default gateway.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The listing was printed or every command succeeded.
.TP
.B 1
A socket could not be created, the interface list could not be read,
an address or MAC argument could not be parsed, an
.BR ioctl (2)
failed while applying a command, or
.B hw
was followed by something other than
.BR ether ,
or
.B inet6
by something other than
.BR add ,
.B del
or
.BR gw .
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/network.conf
Per-interface configuration read by
.IR /etc/rc.d/20-network ,
which calls
.B ifconfig
at boot.
.B ifconfig
itself does not read this file.
.SH EXAMPLES
Show all interfaces:
.PP
.EX
# ifconfig
.EE
.PP
Configure a static IPv4 address and bring the interface up:
.PP
.EX
# ifconfig eth0 10.0.2.15 netmask 255.255.255.0
# ifconfig eth0 gateway 10.0.2.2
# ifconfig eth0 up
.EE
.PP
Add an IPv6 address with a /64 prefix:
.PP
.EX
# ifconfig eth0 inet6 add fec0::3/64
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ioctl (2)
.SH BUGS
Showing a single interface that does not exist prints an error but
still exits with status 0.
.PP
A keyword given without its argument, such as a trailing
.BR netmask ,
is treated as an IPv4 address and rejected with
.BR "invalid IPv4 address" .
.PP
The broadcast address is not derived from the address and netmask
by
.BR ifconfig .
.PP
Only one IPv6 address per interface is displayed.
.PP
Interfaces beyond the first 16 are not listed.
